1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:02,700
Just press a button.

2
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:06,940
The moment is caught, frozen forever.

3
00:00:07,040 --> 00:00:10,840
These images unite us, remind us of our victories,

4
00:00:10,940 --> 00:00:13,940
our tragedies, our dreams.

5
00:00:14,040 --> 00:00:16,320
But at the beginning of the 19th century,

6
00:00:16,420 --> 00:00:20,860
taking a photograph was just that, a dream,

7
00:00:20,960 --> 00:00:23,860
pursued by three very different men.

8
00:00:23,960 --> 00:00:26,360
Now, the invention of photography,

9
00:00:26,361 --> 00:00:29,960
The quest to capture light on Modern Marvels.

10
00:00:51,960 --> 00:00:54,760
The camera, we take it for granted today.

11
00:00:54,761 --> 00:00:57,800
There is a camera in almost every American home.

12
00:00:57,900 --> 00:00:59,700
We don't think twice about how it works.

13
00:00:59,800 --> 00:01:02,400
We just aim, focus, and push a button.

14
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:06,440
This marvelous box brings a wide world into our hands.

15
00:01:06,540 --> 00:01:09,640
But the camera really isn't a very complicated device.

16
00:01:09,740 --> 00:01:11,340
Here's how it works.

17
00:01:11,440 --> 00:01:13,520
When you focus the camera on an image,

18
00:01:13,620 --> 00:01:16,740
light reflected from that image enters the lens.

19
00:01:16,840 --> 00:01:19,720
It is inverted and makes an upside down picture on the film

20
00:01:19,820 --> 00:01:21,420
at the back of the camera.

21
00:01:21,520 --> 00:01:23,260
We drop the film off for development.

22
00:01:23,261 --> 00:01:25,660
It's dipped in chemicals, and a negative image appears

23
00:01:25,760 --> 00:01:27,360
from which pictures are printed.

24
00:01:32,160 --> 00:01:37,300
Strangely enough, a basic camera was used almost 1,000 years ago.

25
00:01:37,400 --> 00:01:39,840
In the 11th century, Arabian astronomers

26
00:01:39,940 --> 00:01:43,580
used a camera to trace a path of the stars.

27
00:01:43,680 --> 00:01:47,220
Their camera was a darkened room with a small hole in one wall.

28
00:01:47,320 --> 00:01:49,160
On the wall opposite the hole, you

29
00:01:49,161 --> 00:01:54,400
you would see projected upside down the very image

30
00:01:54,500 --> 00:01:56,500
of what was outside the window.

31
00:01:56,600 --> 00:01:59,200
And this discovery was called the camera obscura.

32
00:01:59,300 --> 00:02:01,300
The word camera means room.

33
00:02:01,400 --> 00:02:04,000
The word obscura in Italian means dark.

34
00:02:04,100 --> 00:02:07,520
Camera obscuras became popular tools for artists.

35
00:02:07,620 --> 00:02:11,520
It might surprise museum goers to know that by the 18th century,

36
00:02:11,620 --> 00:02:13,960
many famous painters traced their subjects

37
00:02:14,060 --> 00:02:16,920
with the camera obscura.

38
00:02:16,921 --> 00:02:21,360
Art began to call for, to demand that a way be invented,

39
00:02:21,460 --> 00:02:26,400
a way be devised, that the actual world, reality,

40
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:29,240
could be recorded more faithfully than it

41
00:02:29,340 --> 00:02:32,140
had been recorded before.

42
00:02:32,240 --> 00:02:35,400
But the problem with drawing aids like the camera obscura

43
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:38,400
was that if the artist tracing the image wasn't very good,

44
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:40,240
the drawing wasn't very good.

45
00:02:40,340 --> 00:02:42,320
Cameras made a perfect reflection of light,

46
00:02:42,420 --> 00:02:46,920
but how great an achievement to make the image permanent.

47
00:02:46,921 --> 00:02:50,960
The invention of photography would demand more than an artist's

48
00:02:51,060 --> 00:02:52,160
desire.

49
00:02:52,260 --> 00:02:54,860
It would require a scientist's curiosity.

50
00:02:54,960 --> 00:02:57,560
Simply stated, photography could never be possible

51
00:02:57,660 --> 00:03:00,560
without some understanding of chemical reaction to light.

52
00:03:08,580 --> 00:03:11,220
Over 3,000 years ago, early civilizations

53
00:03:11,320 --> 00:03:13,520
noticed that sunlight faded fabrics,

54
00:03:13,620 --> 00:03:15,080
but they didn't know why.

55
00:03:15,081 --> 00:03:18,320
centuries would pass before a very important discovery

56
00:03:18,420 --> 00:03:21,020
was made about chemical sensitivity to light.

57
00:03:21,120 --> 00:03:23,720
In the 1700s, Johann Heinrich Schultz

58
00:03:23,820 --> 00:03:25,520
was working with silver chloride

59
00:03:25,620 --> 00:03:28,560
when he realized that the silver particles changed color

60
00:03:28,660 --> 00:03:30,300
after exposure to sunlight.

61
00:03:32,540 --> 00:03:35,440
And he would make little experiments, almost games,

62
00:03:35,540 --> 00:03:37,100
for his friends, where he would take a flask

63
00:03:37,200 --> 00:03:40,300
and fill it full of this solution of chalk and silver

64
00:03:40,400 --> 00:03:43,000
chloride, and he'd put the stencil on the flask,

65
00:03:43,001 --> 00:03:45,840
hold it in the light for a while, then take the paper away

66
00:03:45,940 --> 00:03:50,800
and the silver, blackened, oxidized in relation to light.

67
00:03:50,900 --> 00:03:52,380
Though he took it no further,

68
00:03:52,480 --> 00:03:54,980
Schultz's discovery would become a basic principle

69
00:03:55,080 --> 00:03:56,280
of photography.

70
00:03:56,380 --> 00:03:58,920
The tools for creating an image were now known.

71
00:04:03,620 --> 00:04:06,720
The early 1800s were a time of prolific invention,

72
00:04:06,820 --> 00:04:08,520
and in the dawn of the Industrial Revolution,

73
00:04:08,620 --> 00:04:11,820
there were great races to patent and protect one's ideas.

74
00:04:11,821 --> 00:04:12,820
ideas.

75
00:04:12,920 --> 00:04:14,920
In central France, a modest inventor

76
00:04:15,020 --> 00:04:17,260
would make a giant contribution to the development

77
00:04:17,360 --> 00:04:19,920
of the still camera, though he was trying to invent something

78
00:04:20,020 --> 00:04:20,920
very different.

79
00:04:21,020 --> 00:04:23,400
In a French village near Paris in 1816,

80
00:04:23,500 --> 00:04:26,540
Joseph Nyssa for Niepce became interested in refining

81
00:04:26,640 --> 00:04:28,740
the new art of lithography in order

82
00:04:28,840 --> 00:04:31,940
to make copies of pre-existing works of art.

83
00:04:32,040 --> 00:04:34,380
But he needed drawings in order to do experiments,

84
00:04:34,480 --> 00:04:37,120
and Niepce couldn't draw for beans.

85
00:04:37,220 --> 00:04:40,020
He decided to make his drawings with the aid of light,

86
00:04:40,021 --> 00:04:44,720
an idea far more difficult than refining lithography.

87
00:04:44,820 --> 00:04:48,800
In fact, it had stumped many great minds before his.

88
00:04:48,900 --> 00:04:51,460
Niepce built a tiny camera obscura,

89
00:04:51,560 --> 00:04:53,960
a little over one inch wide on each side.

90
00:04:54,060 --> 00:04:55,700
He knew about Schultz's experiments

91
00:04:55,800 --> 00:04:57,380
with light-sensitive silver, so he

92
00:04:57,480 --> 00:04:59,600
pinned paper soaked in silver chloride

93
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:01,240
into the back of his camera.

94
00:05:01,340 --> 00:05:03,840
He was thrilled to get an image on the paper,

95
00:05:03,940 --> 00:05:05,680
but the lights and darks were reversed,

96
00:05:05,780 --> 00:05:08,420
and he had no idea how to make a print or normal-looking

97
00:05:08,421 --> 00:05:09,960
picture from it.

98
00:05:10,060 --> 00:05:12,720
Six years of tedious experimentation

99
00:05:12,820 --> 00:05:15,120
passed while he puzzled over better methods

100
00:05:15,220 --> 00:05:18,860
of creating a good image.

101
00:05:18,960 --> 00:05:22,460
In July of 1822, Niepce stumbled upon a solution.

102
00:05:22,560 --> 00:05:26,080
He knew that artists use an asphalt compound in lithography.

103
00:05:26,180 --> 00:05:29,780
Niepce coated a plate with asphalt instead of silver,

104
00:05:29,880 --> 00:05:31,780
placed a translucent image on the plate,

105
00:05:31,880 --> 00:05:33,180
and exposed it to the sun.

106
00:05:33,181 --> 00:05:39,420
It was a radical idea, and it worked.

107
00:05:39,520 --> 00:05:42,820
He could copy works of art.

108
00:05:47,500 --> 00:05:52,640
In the summer of 1826, an even greater triumph would come.

109
00:05:52,740 --> 00:05:55,540
He used a crude camera obscura that he had,

110
00:05:55,640 --> 00:05:57,880
and he put the plate in the back of the camera obscura,

111
00:05:57,980 --> 00:05:59,840
stuck it out the window of his studio,

112
00:05:59,841 --> 00:06:03,020
of his studio and exposed it to the view out there for eight

113
00:06:03,120 --> 00:06:04,020
hours.

114
00:06:04,120 --> 00:06:07,140
The result, what many consider the world's first photograph,

115
00:06:07,240 --> 00:06:10,420
was the view from the attic window of his country house.

116
00:06:10,520 --> 00:06:12,820
Niepce called his invention heliography.

117
00:06:16,560 --> 00:06:19,320
Shortly after his discovery, Niepce received a letter

118
00:06:19,420 --> 00:06:22,120
from a stranger in Paris, who to Niepce's horror

119
00:06:22,220 --> 00:06:24,360
knew about his experiments.

120
00:06:24,460 --> 00:06:26,500
The stranger wanted to know Niepce's secret

121
00:06:26,600 --> 00:06:28,460
in creating heliographs and claimed

122
00:06:28,461 --> 00:06:33,300
that he too was working on making pictures from light.

123
00:06:33,400 --> 00:06:36,460
Niepce was afraid his ideas might be stolen.

124
00:06:36,560 --> 00:06:38,600
He did some nosing around and discovered

125
00:06:38,700 --> 00:06:40,500
that the letter writer who signed his name,

126
00:06:40,600 --> 00:06:44,000
Daguerre, was a respected Parisian artist.

127
00:06:44,100 --> 00:06:47,520
Niepce wrote a courteous reply, but did not reveal his technique.

128
00:06:50,220 --> 00:06:52,020
Shortly after he received the letter,

129
00:06:52,120 --> 00:06:54,820
Niepce was compelled to make a trip to London.

130
00:06:54,920 --> 00:06:57,620
On his stopover in Paris, he decided on impulse

131
00:06:57,621 --> 00:07:02,520
to meet this stranger named Daguerre.

132
00:07:02,620 --> 00:07:05,620
Louis-Jacques-Mond Daguerre was not only an artist,

133
00:07:05,720 --> 00:07:09,360
but the joint owner in a popular gallery called the Diorama.

134
00:07:09,460 --> 00:07:12,260
The Diorama was like Grumman's Chinese Theater

135
00:07:12,360 --> 00:07:14,680
in Hollywood, a place that everybody knew about.

136
00:07:14,780 --> 00:07:16,180
It was a mecca for tourists.

137
00:07:16,280 --> 00:07:18,180
It was a cornucopia of money.

138
00:07:18,280 --> 00:07:22,680
It was highly profitable, and it made him famous.

139
00:07:22,780 --> 00:07:24,680
Spectators watched with amazement

140
00:07:24,780 --> 00:07:26,680
as large pictorial views appeared

141
00:07:26,681 --> 00:07:28,380
to dissolve into other landscapes

142
00:07:28,480 --> 00:07:31,360
through the effect of lights on lightly painted canvas.

143
00:07:31,460 --> 00:07:35,020
Daguerre used a camera obscured to draw correct perspectives

144
00:07:35,120 --> 00:07:37,220
on his huge paintings.

145
00:07:37,320 --> 00:07:39,900
He bought his camera lenses from the same optician who sold

146
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:41,120
lenses to Niepce.

147
00:07:41,220 --> 00:07:43,800
In fact, it was the optician who had told Daguerre

148
00:07:43,900 --> 00:07:46,260
of Niepce's experiments with a camera.

149
00:07:46,360 --> 00:07:51,100
They finally met in January of 1827.

150
00:07:51,200 --> 00:07:52,880
We know Daguerre to be a very charming man.

151
00:07:52,980 --> 00:07:54,640
I mean, part of the theater world.

152
00:07:54,641 --> 00:07:58,080
He knew how to smooth palms and get his own way and so on.

153
00:07:58,180 --> 00:08:00,380
I think Niepce was quite impressed.

154
00:08:00,480 --> 00:08:01,980
Niepce hurried onto London, where

155
00:08:02,080 --> 00:08:04,120
he found that a reversal in family fortune

156
00:08:04,220 --> 00:08:06,380
had almost bankrupted him.

157
00:08:06,480 --> 00:08:09,380
He became even more determined to carry on his experiments

158
00:08:09,480 --> 00:08:13,020
with a camera, and equally determined was Louis Daguerre,

159
00:08:13,120 --> 00:08:14,960
Paris showman.

160
00:08:15,060 --> 00:08:17,500
Niepce and Daguerre would shortly become partners

161
00:08:17,600 --> 00:08:20,540
in a race that would end in greatness for one

162
00:08:20,640 --> 00:08:24,000
and obscurity for the other.

163
00:08:24,001 --> 00:08:26,240
Before cameras, inexpensive portraits

164
00:08:26,340 --> 00:08:28,800
were made by cutting silhouettes, a process

165
00:08:28,900 --> 00:08:31,840
sarcastically named after French finance minister

166
00:08:31,940 --> 00:08:35,380
Etienne de Silhouettes, who had proposed taxing the rich so

167
00:08:35,480 --> 00:08:40,320
heavily that only their outline would remain.

168
00:08:40,420 --> 00:08:43,660
The camera will return in a moment on Modern Marvels.

169
00:08:47,820 --> 00:08:50,800
We now return to the camera on Modern Marvels.

170
00:08:50,801 --> 00:08:55,240
The two men who raced toward the discovery of photography

171
00:08:55,340 --> 00:08:57,300
were a study in contrasts.

172
00:08:57,400 --> 00:09:00,440
Joseph Niepce was modest, polite, and trusting.

173
00:09:00,540 --> 00:09:03,740
Louis Daguerre was a showman and something of a hustler.

174
00:09:03,840 --> 00:09:06,580
Niepce possessed a strong scientific background,

175
00:09:06,680 --> 00:09:09,860
while Daguerre had little formal education.

176
00:09:09,960 --> 00:09:12,380
Even so, Daguerre was obsessed with the idea

177
00:09:12,480 --> 00:09:14,520
of fixing an image in a camera.

178
00:09:14,620 --> 00:09:16,560
But he was hindered in pursuing research

179
00:09:16,660 --> 00:09:19,760
by his ignorance of chemistry and physics.

180
00:09:19,761 --> 00:09:21,600
When he learned of Niepce's experiments,

181
00:09:21,700 --> 00:09:26,340
Daguerre was even more motivated to pursue the same idea.

182
00:09:26,440 --> 00:09:28,540
Daguerre wrote Niepce several charming notes

183
00:09:28,640 --> 00:09:30,500
after their first meeting in Paris.

184
00:09:30,600 --> 00:09:32,340
He spoke of his continuing research

185
00:09:32,440 --> 00:09:34,480
into making images in the camera.

186
00:09:34,580 --> 00:09:35,780
DAGUEZ

187
00:09:35,781 --> 00:09:38,080
In fact, Daguerre, I think, had kind of fooled Niepce into thinking

188
00:09:38,180 --> 00:09:40,120
that he'd got a lot further with his experiments

189
00:09:40,220 --> 00:09:41,620
than he actually had.

190
00:09:41,720 --> 00:09:43,820
At the end of two years in 1829,

191
00:09:43,920 --> 00:09:47,520
Niepce made a new breakthrough and sent the results to Daguerre.

192
00:09:47,521 --> 00:09:51,520
Niepce had succeeded in making a still life in his little camera.

193
00:09:51,620 --> 00:09:54,020
In the accompanying note, Niepce spoke of his intention

194
00:09:54,120 --> 00:09:55,520
to publish his work.

195
00:09:55,620 --> 00:09:57,520
Daguerre believed there was money to be made,

196
00:09:57,620 --> 00:10:00,260
and he wanted a piece of the pie.

197
00:10:00,360 --> 00:10:02,560
He urged Niepce to delay publication

198
00:10:02,660 --> 00:10:05,380
until the process could be perfected.

199
00:10:05,480 --> 00:10:08,080
He also offered his collaboration and claimed

200
00:10:08,180 --> 00:10:11,980
to have a camera lens three times as fast as Niepce's.

201
00:10:12,080 --> 00:10:14,980
That claim would turn out to be a great exaggeration,

202
00:10:14,981 --> 00:10:17,880
but Niepce was convinced by Daguerre's arguments.

203
00:10:17,980 --> 00:10:19,980
He agreed not to publish his findings.

204
00:10:27,960 --> 00:10:31,560
Niepce was now 64 years old, tired and impoverished.

205
00:10:31,660 --> 00:10:33,560
He had worked alone for 13 years,

206
00:10:33,660 --> 00:10:35,760
and he believed he could not advance the work further

207
00:10:35,860 --> 00:10:38,340
without a perfected lens.

208
00:10:38,440 --> 00:10:42,740
Daguerre, in stark contrast, was 42 in the prime of life,

209
00:10:42,741 --> 00:10:45,380
energetic and confident that heliography

210
00:10:45,480 --> 00:10:49,020
could become as successful as the diorama.

211
00:10:49,120 --> 00:10:51,020
It seems to Niepce that it would be a good idea

212
00:10:51,120 --> 00:10:53,880
to hook up with this entrepreneurial younger man

213
00:10:53,980 --> 00:10:55,960
who was already quite a famous figure in Paris

214
00:10:56,060 --> 00:10:57,360
and who was well connected.

215
00:10:57,460 --> 00:10:59,860
But perhaps as a collaboration, this

216
00:10:59,960 --> 00:11:03,220
would be a better way to go than experimenting on his own.

217
00:11:03,320 --> 00:11:05,200
Daguerre traveled to Niepce's home,

218
00:11:05,300 --> 00:11:07,860
and the two men signed a 10-year partnership.

219
00:11:07,960 --> 00:11:10,440
But over the next four years, working separately,

220
00:11:10,441 --> 00:11:13,180
separately, neither man made breakthroughs.

221
00:11:13,280 --> 00:11:18,780
Then in 1833, without warning, Niepce died of a stroke.

222
00:11:18,880 --> 00:11:20,920
His great contribution to photography

223
00:11:21,020 --> 00:11:23,180
remained unpublished.

224
00:11:23,280 --> 00:11:26,720
His debts were so large that his son Isidore was forced

225
00:11:26,820 --> 00:11:29,160
to sell the family estate.

226
00:11:29,260 --> 00:11:32,060
However, Isidore inherited his father's interest

227
00:11:32,160 --> 00:11:34,700
in the partnership with Daguerre.

228
00:11:34,800 --> 00:11:37,300
Two years after Niepce's death, his son Isidore

229
00:11:37,400 --> 00:11:39,840
received an urgent letter from Daguerre.

230
00:11:39,841 --> 00:11:43,180
At long last, Daguerre had made a revolutionary discovery.

231
00:11:43,280 --> 00:11:46,840
He had found a new method to obtain an image from the camera.

232
00:11:46,940 --> 00:11:49,740
Daguerre chemically treated a silvered plate

233
00:11:49,840 --> 00:11:51,980
and placed the plate in the camera.

234
00:11:52,080 --> 00:11:55,080
But instead of leaving the plate there until an image appeared,

235
00:11:55,180 --> 00:11:59,500
he took it out immediately and heated it over mercury vapors.

236
00:11:59,600 --> 00:12:02,600
An image soon developed, what we call a latent image.

237
00:12:02,700 --> 00:12:05,540
It was an astounding finding that would change the course

238
00:12:05,640 --> 00:12:06,940
of photography.

239
00:12:06,941 --> 00:12:10,680
Yet no record remains of what led Daguerre to this discovery.

240
00:12:10,780 --> 00:12:14,320
We like to think that discoveries are done by geniuses

241
00:12:14,420 --> 00:12:17,780
who cry, Eureka, after a moment of flash of inspiration.

242
00:12:17,880 --> 00:12:19,380
But I think, in fact, the invention

243
00:12:19,480 --> 00:12:21,480
was a somewhat gradual one.

244
00:12:21,580 --> 00:12:23,860
Isidore Niepce rushed to Paris.

245
00:12:23,960 --> 00:12:27,000
Daguerre handed Niepce's son a new contract to sign.

246
00:12:27,100 --> 00:12:29,120
It changed the name of the firm from Niepce

247
00:12:29,220 --> 00:12:30,700
Daguerre to Daguerre Niepce.

248
00:12:33,240 --> 00:12:35,900
Daguerre inherited all of the notebooks

249
00:12:35,901 --> 00:12:37,700
and research of his partner.

250
00:12:37,800 --> 00:12:40,540
And those notebooks were valuable and allowed

251
00:12:40,640 --> 00:12:44,680
Daguerre, who was himself an artist and not a scientist,

252
00:12:44,780 --> 00:12:50,520
to actually go ahead and successfully make a photograph

253
00:12:50,620 --> 00:12:52,280
as we know it today.

254
00:12:52,380 --> 00:12:54,160
In his theatrical style,

255
00:12:54,260 --> 00:12:56,460
Daguerre publicly announced his discovery,

256
00:12:56,560 --> 00:12:58,920
but his announcement was premature.

257
00:12:59,020 --> 00:13:00,900
A major problem remained.

258
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:03,000
He could develop an image, but he could not

259
00:13:03,100 --> 00:13:05,020
make that image permanent.

260
00:13:05,021 --> 00:13:06,780
If it was exposed to further sunlight,

261
00:13:06,880 --> 00:13:08,660
the image would fade away.

262
00:13:15,360 --> 00:13:17,340
It would have disturbed De Gehr tremendously

263
00:13:17,440 --> 00:13:20,460
if he had known that he was not the only man closing in

264
00:13:20,560 --> 00:13:22,500
on a solution to this great riddle.

265
00:13:22,600 --> 00:13:26,200
In fact, a 31-year-old Englishman named William Henry Fox Talbot

266
00:13:26,300 --> 00:13:28,680
was working on the very same idea.

267
00:13:28,780 --> 00:13:32,120
On Talbot's honeymoon trip to northern Italy in 1832,

268
00:13:32,220 --> 00:13:33,920
he had tried to use drawing aids,

269
00:13:33,921 --> 00:13:37,360
including the Camera Obscura and another gadget called Camera

270
00:13:37,460 --> 00:13:42,160
Lucida, captured the natural beauty of Lake Como.

271
00:13:42,260 --> 00:13:45,660
Camera Lucida used a prism to reflect the artist's subjects

272
00:13:45,760 --> 00:13:47,460
onto a piece of paper.

273
00:13:47,560 --> 00:13:51,780
Talbot traced the reflection at his hotel and the lake.

274
00:13:51,880 --> 00:13:53,480
And he looked at the drawings that he made.

275
00:13:53,580 --> 00:13:59,820
And because he was a smart man and essentially a pragmatic man,

276
00:13:59,920 --> 00:14:03,280
he said to himself, these drawings are terrible.

277
00:14:03,281 --> 00:14:05,620
These are terrible.

278
00:14:05,720 --> 00:14:07,220
It was a magical moment.

279
00:14:07,320 --> 00:14:11,520
And on that magical moment, he got a brilliant insight

280
00:14:11,620 --> 00:14:15,200
that wouldn't it be extraordinary to be able to make

281
00:14:15,300 --> 00:14:17,700
permanent the drawings that he was making

282
00:14:17,800 --> 00:14:19,200
with the camera that was seated.

283
00:14:19,300 --> 00:14:21,460
Unaware of Neops and Daguerre's work,

284
00:14:21,560 --> 00:14:24,800
Talbot began to experiment with tiny cameras.

285
00:14:24,900 --> 00:14:27,680
We have to realize that Talbot wasn't just a kind of arbitrary

286
00:14:27,780 --> 00:14:28,700
tinkerer.

287
00:14:28,800 --> 00:14:30,240
This was a man who was probably as well-educated

288
00:14:30,340 --> 00:14:32,540
as any man in Europe was at this time.

289
00:14:32,541 --> 00:14:34,880
His strong background in chemistry and optics

290
00:14:34,980 --> 00:14:36,640
guided his experiments.

291
00:14:36,740 --> 00:14:38,680
Aware of Silver's sensitivity to light,

292
00:14:38,780 --> 00:14:42,880
Talbot worked diligently until he achieved a breakthrough.

293
00:14:42,980 --> 00:14:46,780
He soaked paper in a salt solution, then silver nitrate,

294
00:14:46,880 --> 00:14:50,300
and exposed the paper with an object laid on it to the sun.

295
00:14:50,400 --> 00:14:53,100
Talbot was immediately able to produce an image on paper,

296
00:14:53,200 --> 00:14:55,000
which he called a shadow graph.

297
00:15:02,540 --> 00:15:06,540
Immediately, he said, I've discovered how to fix a shadow.

298
00:15:06,640 --> 00:15:08,980
Isn't that a wonderful phrase?

299
00:15:09,080 --> 00:15:10,980
He created a new art.

300
00:15:11,080 --> 00:15:13,180
But Talbot was just beginning.

301
00:15:13,280 --> 00:15:17,160
In 1835, he made an even more important discovery.

302
00:15:17,260 --> 00:15:19,620
In his notebook, he wrote of exposing paper

303
00:15:19,720 --> 00:15:21,820
in his little camera for several hours

304
00:15:21,920 --> 00:15:24,660
to obtain a shadow graph for negative image.

305
00:15:24,760 --> 00:15:26,660
The lights and darks were reversed.

306
00:15:26,760 --> 00:15:29,600
He laid that shadow graph on another chemically treated

307
00:15:29,700 --> 00:15:31,700
piece of paper, exposed it to the sun,

308
00:15:31,701 --> 00:15:33,940
to the sun and created a copy.

309
00:15:34,040 --> 00:15:36,600
The lights and darks were now normal.

310
00:15:36,700 --> 00:15:41,040
It is what we consider a positive or print.

311
00:15:41,140 --> 00:15:43,140
His first print was a latticework window

312
00:15:43,240 --> 00:15:45,340
at Latcock Abbey, his country home.

313
00:15:45,440 --> 00:15:48,180
Amazingly, he did not understand the magnitude

314
00:15:48,280 --> 00:15:49,680
of his discovery.

315
00:15:49,780 --> 00:15:51,680
Talbot set aside the notebooks that contained

316
00:15:51,780 --> 00:15:54,320
his great invention and turned his attention

317
00:15:54,420 --> 00:15:56,220
to other scholarly pursuits.

318
00:16:01,700 --> 00:16:04,940
Less than 200 miles away, Daguerre

319
00:16:05,040 --> 00:16:07,400
continued to work obsessively in his laboratory

320
00:16:07,500 --> 00:16:09,140
across the channel.

321
00:16:09,240 --> 00:16:13,140
He tried chemical after chemical on his silver plates.

322
00:16:13,240 --> 00:16:15,940
Daguerre was determined to fix the image in the camera

323
00:16:16,040 --> 00:16:18,220
so that it would not fade.

324
00:16:18,320 --> 00:16:21,360
He would not leave his lab for days on end.

325
00:16:21,460 --> 00:16:24,160
He neglected his business and his family.

326
00:16:24,260 --> 00:16:27,760
After two more years of struggle, in 1837,

327
00:16:27,761 --> 00:16:32,600
Daguerre announced that he had found the solution.

328
00:16:32,700 --> 00:16:34,560
Daguerre again sent for Isidore.

329
00:16:34,660 --> 00:16:37,100
When Isidore arrived in Paris, Daguerre triumphantly

330
00:16:37,200 --> 00:16:38,900
revealed his discovery.

331
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:41,880
The method Daguerre discovered was oddly simple.

332
00:16:41,980 --> 00:16:43,700
After obtaining an image on the plate,

333
00:16:43,800 --> 00:16:46,240
Daguerre bathed the plate in a salt solution, which

334
00:16:46,340 --> 00:16:49,520
stopped the chemical reaction on the picture.

335
00:16:49,620 --> 00:16:52,580
Joseph Niepce, whose work was critical to Daguerre's discovery,

336
00:16:52,680 --> 00:16:57,760
would only be credited with the earlier chemical process.

337
00:16:57,761 --> 00:17:01,200
The magnificent invention would be named after Daguerre alone.

338
00:17:01,300 --> 00:17:04,460
He called his pictures Daguerreotypes.

339
00:17:04,560 --> 00:17:07,600
Neap's son, Isidore, was furious.

340
00:17:07,700 --> 00:17:10,140
He told a friend, all his conduct

341
00:17:10,240 --> 00:17:12,740
has been nothing but a heap of shameful and despicable

342
00:17:12,840 --> 00:17:14,080
charlatanism.

343
00:17:14,180 --> 00:17:16,740
Isidore felt that his father had been dishonored,

344
00:17:16,840 --> 00:17:19,780
but Daguerre insisted that his photochemical process was

345
00:17:19,880 --> 00:17:21,580
different from Neap's.

346
00:17:21,680 --> 00:17:24,120
In the end, Isidore signed the new contract,

347
00:17:24,220 --> 00:17:26,320
agreeing to all of Daguerre's terms,

348
00:17:26,321 --> 00:17:29,360
in the hope of receiving some compensation if any money

349
00:17:29,460 --> 00:17:30,760
were made.

350
00:17:30,860 --> 00:17:32,900
Daguerre conveyed his incredible findings

351
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:35,000
to the French Academy of Science.

352
00:17:35,100 --> 00:17:38,100
The Academy announced Daguerre's discovery, though not

353
00:17:38,200 --> 00:17:41,140
in detail, to the world in January of 1839.

354
00:17:47,540 --> 00:17:50,680
In the previous four years, the Englishman William Talbot

355
00:17:50,780 --> 00:17:54,540
had experimented no further with his camera.

356
00:17:54,541 --> 00:17:56,340
His mind was far from his shadow graphs

357
00:17:56,440 --> 00:17:59,020
when he read of Daguerre's invention in the London Times

358
00:17:59,120 --> 00:18:02,180
two weeks after the invention had been announced.

359
00:18:02,280 --> 00:18:05,020
Talbot was horrified that Daguerre was receiving credit

360
00:18:05,120 --> 00:18:09,580
for a process Talbot believed he himself had discovered.

361
00:18:09,680 --> 00:18:13,320
He immediately resurrected his old experiments,

362
00:18:13,420 --> 00:18:15,360
made a few new ones.

363
00:18:15,460 --> 00:18:20,840
And two weeks later, on January 25th of 1839, which

364
00:18:20,940 --> 00:18:23,840
is what I call the birthday of photography,

365
00:18:23,841 --> 00:18:27,740
Talbot made a presentation to a group of people,

366
00:18:27,840 --> 00:18:31,620
to a group mostly scientists in London,

367
00:18:31,720 --> 00:18:34,120
where the first public exhibition of photographs

368
00:18:34,220 --> 00:18:35,020
was made.

369
00:18:37,260 --> 00:18:39,280
Talbot's contributions in developing

370
00:18:39,380 --> 00:18:42,420
a negative, positive process were critical to photography

371
00:18:42,520 --> 00:18:43,960
as it is used today.

372
00:18:44,060 --> 00:18:46,800
But those contributions would bring him no financial gain,

373
00:18:46,900 --> 00:18:49,360
little reward, and a great deal of trouble.

374
00:18:49,460 --> 00:18:50,500
Daguerre, on the other hand, would

375
00:18:50,501 --> 00:18:54,100
would be the one to gain great fame.

376
00:18:54,200 --> 00:18:57,300
In August 1839, Daguerre's photographic process

377
00:18:57,400 --> 00:18:59,300
was announced to the French Academy.

378
00:18:59,400 --> 00:19:01,240
The audience was thrilled.

379
00:19:01,340 --> 00:19:04,240
Within the hour, men rushed to the nearest chemist shops

380
00:19:04,340 --> 00:19:07,860
to purchase materials and try to make daguerreotypes.

381
00:19:07,960 --> 00:19:11,620
The French government awarded Daguerre an annuity of 7,000

382
00:19:11,720 --> 00:19:15,200
francs and to Niep's son Isidore 5,000.

383
00:19:15,300 --> 00:19:19,500
The daguerreotypes soon became enormously popular.

384
00:19:19,501 --> 00:19:21,900
They printed handbooks and manuals describing

385
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:23,440
how to make a daguerreotype.

386
00:19:23,540 --> 00:19:25,640
And these handbooks and manuals were translated

387
00:19:25,740 --> 00:19:27,740
into a dozen languages immediately,

388
00:19:27,840 --> 00:19:29,240
shipped around the world.

389
00:19:29,340 --> 00:19:32,220
And very quickly, daguerreotypes were being

390
00:19:32,320 --> 00:19:36,260
made in practically every city that was itself also

391
00:19:36,360 --> 00:19:37,960
a trading port.

392
00:19:38,060 --> 00:19:39,680
The government encouraged

393
00:19:39,780 --> 00:19:41,020
Daguerre to continue his research,

394
00:19:41,120 --> 00:19:43,960
but he knew his greatest achievement was behind him.

395
00:19:44,060 --> 00:19:46,520
He retired to a small city outside Paris,

396
00:19:46,620 --> 00:19:48,400
where he puttered in his garden.

397
00:19:48,401 --> 00:19:51,200
Daguerre died of a heart attack in 1851.

398
00:19:51,300 --> 00:19:53,980
He was 64 years old.

399
00:19:54,080 --> 00:19:58,140
So Daguerre is the ancestor of one line of photography,

400
00:19:58,240 --> 00:20:01,520
which is instant photography, which is the Polaroid process

401
00:20:01,620 --> 00:20:03,620
and all processes like it.

402
00:20:03,720 --> 00:20:05,420
The world was ready to receive

403
00:20:05,520 --> 00:20:06,660
Daguerre's invention.

404
00:20:06,760 --> 00:20:09,160
The Gazette de France predicted photography

405
00:20:09,260 --> 00:20:14,220
would revolutionize the art of drawing, but problems remained.

406
00:20:14,320 --> 00:20:16,560
Exposure times were unacceptably slow.

407
00:20:16,561 --> 00:20:19,800
The equipment was bulky and impractical, the chemicals

408
00:20:19,900 --> 00:20:21,000
noxious.

409
00:20:21,100 --> 00:20:25,200
Great minds went to work to find the solutions.

410
00:20:25,300 --> 00:20:28,300
The term photography means to draw with light.

411
00:20:28,400 --> 00:20:31,600
It comes from the Greek phos meaning light and graphene

412
00:20:31,700 --> 00:20:34,420
meaning to draw.

413
00:20:34,520 --> 00:20:37,820
The camera will return in a moment on Modern Marvels.

414
00:20:41,920 --> 00:20:45,420
We now return to the camera on Modern Marvels.

415
00:20:46,560 --> 00:20:50,560
Within the first year of Daguerre and Talbot's discoveries,

416
00:20:50,660 --> 00:20:53,560
amateur photographers began carting their heavy equipment

417
00:20:53,660 --> 00:20:55,560
to the far reaches of the globe.

418
00:20:55,660 --> 00:20:58,000
Photography had arrived.

419
00:20:58,100 --> 00:21:02,100
From Athens to Egypt, exotic and faraway places

420
00:21:02,200 --> 00:21:04,320
were captured and brought home.

421
00:21:04,420 --> 00:21:07,420
But photography was not a hobby for the weak of heart.

422
00:21:07,520 --> 00:21:09,920
Each glass plate used in a daguerreotype camera

423
00:21:10,020 --> 00:21:13,360
could easily weigh a pound or more depending on its size.

424
00:21:13,460 --> 00:21:15,560
Chemicals in sealed glass bottles,

425
00:21:15,561 --> 00:21:20,560
heavy tripods and other paraphernalia made the equipment awkward and unwieldy.

426
00:21:20,561 --> 00:21:25,560
In France, there was great enthusiasm for Daguerre's marvelous new invention,

427
00:21:25,561 --> 00:21:30,560
but the public soon grew disappointed with photography's limitations in making portraits.

428
00:21:30,561 --> 00:21:36,560
Cruel-looking metal stands were designed with arms that clamped the poser's head in place.

429
00:21:36,561 --> 00:21:41,560
The subject was required to sit perfectly still for as long as a quarter of an hour,

430
00:21:41,561 --> 00:21:43,960
are usually in direct glaring sunlight

431
00:21:44,060 --> 00:21:46,500
while his portrait was being taken.

432
00:21:46,600 --> 00:21:48,760
One satirical magazine of the time

433
00:21:48,860 --> 00:21:50,960
made fun of Daguerre's process.

434
00:21:51,060 --> 00:21:53,000
You want to make a portrait of your wife?

435
00:21:53,100 --> 00:21:55,400
You fix her head in a temporary iron collar

436
00:21:55,500 --> 00:21:58,000
to get the indispensable immobility.

437
00:21:58,100 --> 00:22:00,020
You point the lens of the camera at her face,

438
00:22:00,120 --> 00:22:03,120
and when you take the portrait, it doesn't represent your wife.

439
00:22:03,220 --> 00:22:07,120
It is her parrot or watering pot or worse.

440
00:22:07,220 --> 00:22:08,720
The difficulty in taking portraits

441
00:22:08,721 --> 00:22:11,660
was just one drawback in early photography.

442
00:22:11,760 --> 00:22:14,620
If a plate was developed incorrectly, the image faded.

443
00:22:14,720 --> 00:22:17,020
Papers used for printing were often flawed.

444
00:22:17,120 --> 00:22:19,000
The chemicals stank and were toxic,

445
00:22:19,100 --> 00:22:20,760
but there was money to be made.

446
00:22:20,860 --> 00:22:25,500
Incentive to improve the invention was very strong.

447
00:22:25,600 --> 00:22:27,340
William Talbot returned to the research

448
00:22:27,440 --> 00:22:29,140
he had abandoned four years earlier.

449
00:22:29,240 --> 00:22:32,080
He knew his original process was slow and imperfect.

450
00:22:32,180 --> 00:22:34,380
Soon he came up with an important improvement.

451
00:22:34,480 --> 00:22:37,180
By exposing wet instead of dry paper in the camera

452
00:22:37,181 --> 00:22:39,940
for a short time, he could develop an image on the paper

453
00:22:40,040 --> 00:22:42,520
afterwards through further chemical treatment.

454
00:22:42,620 --> 00:22:45,220
The process was faster and the image far sharper.

455
00:22:45,320 --> 00:22:47,660
Talbot named photographs made by this process

456
00:22:47,760 --> 00:22:50,420
calotypes, Greek for beautiful pictures.

457
00:22:50,520 --> 00:22:52,920
His mother advised him to call them talotypes

458
00:22:53,020 --> 00:22:56,040
and did so herself until the day she died.

459
00:22:56,140 --> 00:22:57,640
With this faster chemical process,

460
00:22:57,740 --> 00:23:00,040
Talbot could not make portraits.

461
00:23:00,140 --> 00:23:02,780
His 1840 photograph of his wife, Constance,

462
00:23:02,880 --> 00:23:06,140
is one of the oldest surviving calotype portraits.

463
00:23:06,141 --> 00:23:10,480
She sat for it only five stiff minutes.

464
00:23:10,580 --> 00:23:12,280
Over the next two decades, Talbot

465
00:23:12,380 --> 00:23:15,120
continued to make improvements in developing and fixing

466
00:23:15,220 --> 00:23:16,420
photographs.

467
00:23:16,520 --> 00:23:18,780
But he was never to enjoy financial success

468
00:23:18,880 --> 00:23:23,160
from his invention, though he tried to protect his ideas.

469
00:23:23,260 --> 00:23:26,420
Talbot did the unusual.

470
00:23:26,520 --> 00:23:31,140
Instead of sharing with the world at large his discovery

471
00:23:31,240 --> 00:23:34,940
as a miracle that had been given by nature or by God,

472
00:23:34,941 --> 00:23:36,780
He patented the process.

473
00:23:36,880 --> 00:23:39,380
He became embroiled in a number of nasty lawsuits

474
00:23:39,480 --> 00:23:42,140
over others' rights to use his processes.

475
00:23:42,240 --> 00:23:44,180
He lost most of the suits.

476
00:23:44,280 --> 00:23:48,280
Later in his life, Talbot would give up photography entirely.

477
00:23:48,380 --> 00:23:52,300
In 1877, he died of heart failure at Latcock Abbey.

478
00:23:52,400 --> 00:23:54,560
In several obituaries, his contribution

479
00:23:54,660 --> 00:23:58,760
to the invention of photography was barely mentioned.

480
00:23:58,860 --> 00:24:01,260
Of course, there was always rivalry between France and Britain

481
00:24:01,360 --> 00:24:03,500
about which of them had come up with the invention.

482
00:24:03,501 --> 00:24:05,300
But Talbot has been widely recognized

483
00:24:05,400 --> 00:24:08,640
as the founding figure for the kind of photography we use

484
00:24:08,740 --> 00:24:11,740
today, paper-based, negative, positive photography.

485
00:24:11,840 --> 00:24:13,940
In that respect, Talbot is definitely the founding figure.

486
00:24:18,840 --> 00:24:21,520
As portraits became possible, public demand for them

487
00:24:21,620 --> 00:24:26,020
grew quickly and a wide variety of people.

488
00:24:26,120 --> 00:24:27,360
Though it may seem strange today,

489
00:24:27,460 --> 00:24:30,260
requests for portraits of family members who had died

490
00:24:30,360 --> 00:24:32,120
were not uncommon.

491
00:24:32,121 --> 00:24:35,060
Death rates in the 1800s were very high, especially

492
00:24:35,160 --> 00:24:36,260
among children.

493
00:24:36,360 --> 00:24:39,120
Sometimes the dead were propped up in chairs or their bodies

494
00:24:39,220 --> 00:24:41,160
positioned in lifelike poses.

495
00:24:41,260 --> 00:24:43,860
This practice was discontinued by the turn of the century

496
00:24:43,960 --> 00:24:46,600
when, for health reasons, Austria and other countries

497
00:24:46,700 --> 00:24:49,140
finally banned families from taking dead children

498
00:24:49,240 --> 00:24:50,300
to portrait studios.

499
00:24:54,180 --> 00:24:55,580
In the United States, daguerreotype

500
00:24:55,680 --> 00:24:57,780
portraits were extremely popular.

501
00:24:57,880 --> 00:25:02,120
In the 1850s, galleries sprang up everywhere.

502
00:25:02,121 --> 00:25:10,040
Well, it's hard for 20th century people to realize quite how amazing it was to experience

503
00:25:10,041 --> 00:25:12,600
a daguerreotype for the first time, so in the 1840s.

504
00:25:12,601 --> 00:25:16,180
They came in these beautiful leather packages like a jewel, a piece of jewelry.

505
00:25:16,181 --> 00:25:20,080
And in fact, when you opened up and you saw the image, which of course is on a silver

506
00:25:20,081 --> 00:25:24,000
plate, it flashes at you like a beautiful jewel.

507
00:25:24,001 --> 00:25:31,960
Celebrities, judges, ordinary folks, and presidents all sat for

508
00:25:31,961 --> 00:25:36,960
In 1864, Abraham Lincoln posed for the American portraitist

509
00:25:37,060 --> 00:25:38,600
Matthew Brady.

510
00:25:38,700 --> 00:25:40,600
We all have a copy of that photograph,

511
00:25:40,700 --> 00:25:42,100
and most of us don't know it.

512
00:25:42,200 --> 00:25:45,500
It's the portrait on the $5 bill.

513
00:25:45,600 --> 00:25:48,840
Just as quickly as portrait photography became possible,

514
00:25:48,940 --> 00:25:51,240
erotica became popular.

515
00:25:51,340 --> 00:25:54,620
As with painting and sculpture, photographers immediately

516
00:25:54,720 --> 00:25:58,460
began to pose nudes both for artistic and pornographic

517
00:25:58,560 --> 00:25:59,260
purposes.

518
00:26:01,960 --> 00:26:06,960
From 1840 to the early 1860s, the daguerreotype

519
00:26:07,060 --> 00:26:09,360
had a glorious run.

520
00:26:09,460 --> 00:26:13,400
But by the mid-1860s, its day had passed.

521
00:26:13,500 --> 00:26:16,300
The plates were so fragile they had to be sealed under glass,

522
00:26:16,400 --> 00:26:20,380
protected from damaging fingerprints or oxidation.

523
00:26:20,480 --> 00:26:22,580
Daguerreotypes did not duplicate well,

524
00:26:22,680 --> 00:26:24,420
and a portrait by a reputable salon

525
00:26:24,520 --> 00:26:28,520
could cost as much as $1,000 at today's prices.

526
00:26:28,620 --> 00:26:31,960
But more importantly, something came along to do

527
00:26:31,961 --> 00:26:32,960
take their place.

528
00:26:36,540 --> 00:26:38,640
Frederick Scott Archer, an English sculptor,

529
00:26:38,641 --> 00:26:41,360
made an improvement in photography so significant

530
00:26:41,361 --> 00:26:44,640
that most photographers abandoned both the daguerreotype

531
00:26:44,641 --> 00:26:46,920
and the callotype in its favor.

532
00:26:46,921 --> 00:26:49,520
Archer called this process collodion.

533
00:26:49,521 --> 00:26:52,120
He coated glass plates with a compound made of gun,

534
00:26:52,121 --> 00:26:53,460
cotton, and ether.

535
00:26:53,461 --> 00:26:56,000
Unlimited prints could be made from the glass plates,

536
00:26:56,001 --> 00:26:58,580
and the images were pin sharp.

537
00:26:58,581 --> 00:27:01,760
The collodion process would dominate until the 1880s,

538
00:27:01,761 --> 00:27:04,160
when dry plates would replace it.

539
00:27:04,161 --> 00:27:06,360
But despite these improvements, photography

540
00:27:06,361 --> 00:27:11,460
continued to be a cumbersome and elusive hobby.

541
00:27:11,461 --> 00:27:15,600
The early chemistry was, for the most part, fairly toxic.

542
00:27:15,601 --> 00:27:23,720
People became ill, deranged, and otherwise sick

543
00:27:23,721 --> 00:27:26,660
from constant exposure to different kind

544
00:27:26,661 --> 00:27:31,760
of photographic processes and fumes.

545
00:27:31,761 --> 00:27:35,100
Revolutionary advances would have to wait for visionaries

546
00:27:35,200 --> 00:27:37,660
to rethink the camera itself.

547
00:27:37,760 --> 00:27:39,660
One young man would claim that vision

548
00:27:39,760 --> 00:27:42,400
and be responsible for the prototype of what we think

549
00:27:42,500 --> 00:27:45,300
of as the camera today.

550
00:27:45,400 --> 00:27:47,800
Identifying criminals with photographs

551
00:27:47,900 --> 00:27:51,820
was popular with law enforcement and collectors.

552
00:27:51,920 --> 00:27:56,520
In 1876, photographers sold over 50,000 souvenir prints

553
00:27:56,620 --> 00:27:59,120
and sold their own photographs.

554
00:27:59,121 --> 00:28:03,320
over 50,000 souvenir prints of Jesse James Gang

555
00:28:03,420 --> 00:28:06,120
after they were killed in a bank robbery attempt.

556
00:28:06,220 --> 00:28:09,860
The camera will return in a moment on Modern Marvels.

557
00:28:13,660 --> 00:28:18,080
We now return to the camera on Modern Marvels.

558
00:28:18,180 --> 00:28:21,080
Photography was not an invention born in perfection,

559
00:28:21,180 --> 00:28:23,380
but by its second decade, many hobbyists

560
00:28:23,480 --> 00:28:24,920
had improved the process.

561
00:28:25,020 --> 00:28:28,620
Exposure times were shorter, lenses faster.

562
00:28:28,621 --> 00:28:32,060
In the next 20 years, even more astonishing changes would come,

563
00:28:32,160 --> 00:28:35,420
and they would allow the camera to be used in surprising ways.

564
00:28:36,620 --> 00:28:40,300
Camera bodies were designed in a wide array of shapes and configurations.

565
00:28:41,540 --> 00:28:45,920
Some cameras were designed to use both daguerreotype and callotype backs.

566
00:28:46,980 --> 00:28:51,520
Others had an accordion bellows so that the subject could be kept in focus at various distances.

567
00:28:52,220 --> 00:28:55,440
Studio cameras varied in size from small to bigger than a man.

568
00:28:56,380 --> 00:28:58,560
Stereoscopic cameras became very popular.

569
00:28:58,620 --> 00:29:01,620
Pared images viewed through a stereoscope became fused

570
00:29:01,720 --> 00:29:05,060
into one image in the mind and gave a startling illusion

571
00:29:05,160 --> 00:29:07,620
of three dimensions.

572
00:29:07,720 --> 00:29:10,260
Many inventors focused on ways to make photography

573
00:29:10,360 --> 00:29:11,460
more profitable.

574
00:29:11,560 --> 00:29:14,860
Andre Desderry, a Parisian, designed an ingenious camera

575
00:29:14,960 --> 00:29:18,200
in 1854 that allowed up to eight exposures or negatives

576
00:29:18,300 --> 00:29:20,480
to be taken on one plate.

577
00:29:20,580 --> 00:29:23,620
He called them cas de visite, or calling cards.

578
00:29:23,720 --> 00:29:27,180
They were wildly popular and cheap to make.

579
00:29:27,181 --> 00:29:30,820
Photographs of famous people like Princess Alexandra of Wales

580
00:29:30,920 --> 00:29:32,180
sold like hotcakes.

581
00:29:40,520 --> 00:29:42,220
In the second decade of photography,

582
00:29:42,320 --> 00:29:45,740
a broad world was captured with a range of cameras.

583
00:29:45,840 --> 00:29:50,140
But one subject had not yet been chronicled, the arena of war.

584
00:29:50,240 --> 00:29:53,680
In 1855, the Crimean War was raging on the north coast

585
00:29:53,780 --> 00:29:55,040
of the Black Sea.

586
00:29:55,041 --> 00:29:58,140
Roger Fenton, an English lawyer turned photographer,

587
00:29:58,240 --> 00:30:01,640
set sail for Balaclava Harbor with 36 crates

588
00:30:01,740 --> 00:30:05,320
of photographic equipment weighing thousands of pounds.

589
00:30:05,420 --> 00:30:07,980
His plates were so slow Fenton could only film

590
00:30:08,080 --> 00:30:10,080
before or after battle.

591
00:30:10,180 --> 00:30:13,420
He developed his pictures in intensely sweltering heat

592
00:30:13,520 --> 00:30:17,800
in a wine merchant's carriage he had converted into a dark room.

593
00:30:17,900 --> 00:30:19,840
His war photographs shocked people.

594
00:30:22,540 --> 00:30:23,840
A few years later, Americans would get the

595
00:30:23,841 --> 00:30:27,340
Americans would get the same dose of reality.

596
00:30:27,440 --> 00:30:30,080
From the battlefield of Gettysburg to Cold Harbor,

597
00:30:30,180 --> 00:30:32,280
Fredericksburg to Chancellorsville,

598
00:30:32,380 --> 00:30:34,320
Matthew Brady and other cameramen

599
00:30:34,420 --> 00:30:37,420
would yield heavy wagons of photographic equipment

600
00:30:37,520 --> 00:30:42,360
close behind the battle lines, and sometimes inadvertently

601
00:30:42,460 --> 00:30:45,020
on the battle lines.

602
00:30:45,120 --> 00:30:47,800
Their photographs documented with great eloquence

603
00:30:47,900 --> 00:30:51,460
the dead, the dying, and every phase

604
00:30:51,461 --> 00:30:53,260
of the American Civil War.

605
00:31:00,260 --> 00:31:01,800
In the decade after the Civil War,

606
00:31:01,900 --> 00:31:04,100
the development of dry plates and the invention

607
00:31:04,200 --> 00:31:06,200
of fast mechanical shutters finally

608
00:31:06,300 --> 00:31:09,320
allowed photographers to record motion accurately.

609
00:31:09,420 --> 00:31:12,120
That would turn out to be highly profitable for Leland

610
00:31:12,220 --> 00:31:13,220
Stanford.

611
00:31:13,320 --> 00:31:15,320
Stanford, a former California governor and a racehorse

612
00:31:15,420 --> 00:31:19,560
enthusiast, made a $20,000 bet with a friend in 1873

613
00:31:19,561 --> 00:31:22,100
that all four of the horses' hooves left the ground

614
00:31:22,200 --> 00:31:24,000
at the same time during a gallop,

615
00:31:24,100 --> 00:31:27,060
but not in the manner in which artists depicted them.

616
00:31:27,160 --> 00:31:29,500
Stanford turned to a camera to prove his theory.

617
00:31:29,600 --> 00:31:32,180
Stanford hired Edward Muybridge, a British photographer

618
00:31:32,280 --> 00:31:35,000
living in San Francisco, to settle the question.

619
00:31:35,100 --> 00:31:37,780
Motivated by his fascination with motion photography,

620
00:31:37,880 --> 00:31:40,420
Muybridge had invented a fast mechanical shutter.

621
00:31:40,520 --> 00:31:44,020
In 1877, Muybridge lined 24 cameras in a row,

622
00:31:44,120 --> 00:31:46,260
a few paces apart on the track at Leland's Farm

623
00:31:46,360 --> 00:31:48,920
in Palo Alto, California.

624
00:31:48,921 --> 00:31:53,220
Each camera shutter mechanism was controlled by a string.

625
00:31:53,320 --> 00:31:55,360
And as the horse went down the track,

626
00:31:55,460 --> 00:31:57,420
it broke the string and clicked the shutter.

627
00:31:57,520 --> 00:32:01,600
And you got an image of a horse in that particular position.

628
00:32:01,700 --> 00:32:03,240
The photograph showed beyond a doubt

629
00:32:03,340 --> 00:32:05,740
that a horse's hooves did indeed all come off the ground

630
00:32:05,840 --> 00:32:09,540
at the same time in his gallop and in an unlikely place,

631
00:32:09,640 --> 00:32:11,240
under the horse's belly.

632
00:32:11,340 --> 00:32:12,980
Stanford won his bet.

633
00:32:13,080 --> 00:32:15,220
Muybridge's shots of progressive action

634
00:32:15,320 --> 00:32:17,480
heralded a new direction in photography

635
00:32:17,481 --> 00:32:20,480
as with the ideas of a young man in upstate New York.

636
00:32:30,020 --> 00:32:32,460
A young bank clerk in Rochester, New York,

637
00:32:32,461 --> 00:32:35,560
took up photography as a hobby in the 1870s.

638
00:32:35,561 --> 00:32:38,060
The clerk's name was George Eastman.

639
00:32:38,061 --> 00:32:41,000
Eastman's father had died when the boy was 14,

640
00:32:41,001 --> 00:32:44,040
and poverty forced George to go to work.

641
00:32:44,041 --> 00:32:46,040
Eastman loved tinkering.

642
00:32:46,041 --> 00:32:49,380
By his 20s, his routine was to work in the bank during the day

643
00:32:49,381 --> 00:32:51,280
and in his photography lab at night.

644
00:32:51,281 --> 00:32:53,880
He was determined to invent a dry film emulsion

645
00:32:53,881 --> 00:32:55,980
made of gelatin, which hobbyists thought

646
00:32:55,981 --> 00:32:59,760
would be more practical than the chemicals in use at the time.

647
00:32:59,761 --> 00:33:01,360
After some months of experimenting,

648
00:33:01,361 --> 00:33:04,060
he came up with a workable emulsion.

649
00:33:04,061 --> 00:33:08,620
He designed a coating machine to apply it to the plate.

650
00:33:08,621 --> 00:33:10,400
Eastman took money from his savings

651
00:33:10,401 --> 00:33:13,100
and sailed for England, where he applied for patents for both

652
00:33:13,101 --> 00:33:15,700
his emulsion and coating machine.

653
00:33:15,701 --> 00:33:18,040
The emulsion sold modestly well.

654
00:33:18,041 --> 00:33:20,740
But more importantly, Eastman was building a reputation

655
00:33:20,741 --> 00:33:22,240
for reliability.

656
00:33:22,241 --> 00:33:24,840
That reputation would be important when he embarked

657
00:33:24,841 --> 00:33:27,520
on a new pursuit, designing a flexible base

658
00:33:27,521 --> 00:33:30,180
to replace the photographic plate.

659
00:33:30,181 --> 00:33:33,920
In 1884, after two more years of constant experimentation,

660
00:33:33,921 --> 00:33:37,520
Eastman began selling a paper base he called film.

661
00:33:37,521 --> 00:33:40,820
He also designed a holder from which to roll the paper.

662
00:33:40,821 --> 00:33:43,120
Film's weight and flexibility were wonderful,

663
00:33:43,121 --> 00:33:45,700
but developing it was tricky because the emulsion

664
00:33:45,701 --> 00:33:47,720
had to be carefully peeled from the base.

665
00:33:48,160 --> 00:33:50,040
It also sold modestly.

666
00:33:51,840 --> 00:33:54,720
Eastman continued to develop new ideas and to market them.

667
00:33:55,140 --> 00:33:59,020
In 1888, he sailed to England again to patent a trademark name

668
00:33:59,021 --> 00:34:01,280
he'd been mulling over for quite some time.

669
00:34:01,900 --> 00:34:04,840
He was trying to find a name that was incredibly memorable,

670
00:34:05,060 --> 00:34:07,480
that you couldn't forget, you couldn't confuse it with anything else,

671
00:34:08,020 --> 00:34:12,140
that would satisfy all patent laws in a variety of different countries,

672
00:34:12,141 --> 00:34:15,160
and which he himself said, because of the two consonants, Kodak,

673
00:34:15,161 --> 00:34:17,340
It sounded like the click of a camera shutter.

674
00:34:17,460 --> 00:34:19,220
So that would always remind you of, in fact,

675
00:34:19,280 --> 00:34:20,840
what the company was associated with.

676
00:34:21,040 --> 00:34:24,080
The first product Eastman introduced under the name Kodak

677
00:34:24,081 --> 00:34:25,960
would change the course of photography.

678
00:34:26,520 --> 00:34:30,720
It was a camera half the size of a shoebox, 22 ounces in weight.

679
00:34:31,240 --> 00:34:33,840
It wasn't the first small camera by any means.

680
00:34:34,300 --> 00:34:36,060
Smaller camera bodies had been introduced

681
00:34:36,061 --> 00:34:38,300
by various companies in the 1880s.

682
00:34:38,680 --> 00:34:41,100
But Eastman added something ingenious,

683
00:34:41,101 --> 00:34:46,840
a 50-foot roll of film capable of making 100 exposures.

684
00:34:46,940 --> 00:34:50,100
Loaded with film, the camera cost a steep $25.

685
00:34:50,200 --> 00:34:53,080
But Eastman's stroke of genius was that for $10,

686
00:34:53,180 --> 00:34:57,020
his company would process the film, reload the camera,

687
00:34:57,120 --> 00:35:01,880
and return the camera and prints to the customer.

688
00:35:01,980 --> 00:35:04,520
He marketed this whole process under the slogan,

689
00:35:04,620 --> 00:35:06,420
you press the button, we do the rest.

690
00:35:06,520 --> 00:35:08,420
And this was his, in a way, he revolutionized

691
00:35:08,520 --> 00:35:09,560
the whole business.

692
00:35:09,561 --> 00:35:12,400
because the most unskilled amateur could now take photographs.

693
00:35:13,040 --> 00:35:16,220
The Kodak camera went on the market in July of 1888.

694
00:35:16,640 --> 00:35:20,000
In six months, to Eastman's astonishment, he had sold 2,500

695
00:35:20,001 --> 00:35:22,960
and set a course for the future of photography.

696
00:35:24,820 --> 00:35:30,260
In the next century, Kodak cameras would be offered in every shape, size, and color imaginable.

697
00:35:30,760 --> 00:35:33,420
A camera marketed for children was called the Brownie.

698
00:35:33,860 --> 00:35:36,820
The camera targeted at women included a lipstick and compact.

699
00:35:36,821 --> 00:35:40,160
Eastman's company would reach into every arena of amateur

700
00:35:40,260 --> 00:35:42,060
and professional photography.

701
00:35:42,160 --> 00:35:45,560
Kodak became the household name in photographic supplies,

702
00:35:45,660 --> 00:35:47,460
and it is to this day.

703
00:35:47,560 --> 00:35:50,460
Eastman's real genius lay in his business sense.

704
00:35:50,560 --> 00:35:53,340
His advertising campaigns were brilliant.

705
00:35:53,440 --> 00:35:56,180
His advertising constantly played on ideas of memory

706
00:35:56,280 --> 00:35:57,680
and desire and loss.

707
00:35:57,780 --> 00:35:59,580
The idea that if one didn't take a photograph,

708
00:35:59,680 --> 00:36:00,780
one would lose this memory.

709
00:36:00,880 --> 00:36:04,580
And with it, your entire child's childhood, if you like.

710
00:36:04,581 --> 00:36:07,080
Eastman's pioneering achievements in photography

711
00:36:07,180 --> 00:36:09,880
would extend to other mediums as well.

712
00:36:09,980 --> 00:36:13,060
His work in film was in no small measure responsible

713
00:36:13,160 --> 00:36:16,560
for an invention on another frontier, the moving picture.

714
00:36:23,060 --> 00:36:26,160
New lenses, new cameras, and a new century

715
00:36:26,260 --> 00:36:29,840
would bring great changes to the way people use photography.

716
00:36:29,940 --> 00:36:31,980
But a large disappointment remained,

717
00:36:31,981 --> 00:36:34,820
how to turn a black and white world into color.

718
00:36:37,780 --> 00:36:40,820
An early advertisement for the Kodak camera boasted,

719
00:36:40,920 --> 00:36:44,200
it is now easy for any person of ordinary intelligence

720
00:36:44,300 --> 00:36:48,560
to learn to take good photographs in 10 minutes.

721
00:36:48,660 --> 00:36:50,500
Modern Marvels will return.

722
00:36:55,300 --> 00:36:57,640
We now return to Modern Marvels.

723
00:36:57,641 --> 00:37:00,780
marvels.

724
00:37:00,880 --> 00:37:03,480
As the 19th century drew to a close,

725
00:37:03,580 --> 00:37:06,640
the evolution of the camera and of chemical processes

726
00:37:06,740 --> 00:37:11,820
allowed photography to expand further into science and business.

727
00:37:11,920 --> 00:37:14,220
Police work, x-rays, and news photography

728
00:37:14,320 --> 00:37:16,520
were just some of its uses.

729
00:37:16,620 --> 00:37:19,320
People readily accepted photography's usefulness,

730
00:37:19,420 --> 00:37:22,240
but few accepted it as art.

731
00:37:22,340 --> 00:37:27,300
In fact, most early photographers did not see themselves as artists.

732
00:37:27,301 --> 00:37:29,840
But a few visionaries like Julia Margaret Cameron

733
00:37:29,940 --> 00:37:32,440
took photography to a higher level.

734
00:37:32,540 --> 00:37:37,340
In the 1860s, Cameron set out to create portraits that were art.

735
00:37:37,440 --> 00:37:39,740
She used dramatic lighting and borrowed ideas

736
00:37:39,840 --> 00:37:41,040
from the classics.

737
00:37:41,140 --> 00:37:43,240
She converted a chicken coop into a dark room

738
00:37:43,340 --> 00:37:46,760
and composed personal dramatic portraits of her friends.

739
00:37:46,860 --> 00:37:50,860
Among her subjects were the scientist Darwin, the poet

740
00:37:50,960 --> 00:37:55,360
Tennyson, and the Alice who as a child had inspired Lewis Carroll's

741
00:37:55,361 --> 00:37:56,600
Alice in Wonderland.

742
00:37:57,420 --> 00:38:01,700
I think she is one of the greatest of geniuses

743
00:38:01,701 --> 00:38:06,060
in the 19th century and in the whole history of photography.

744
00:38:06,480 --> 00:38:08,680
Critics mocked her soft focus.

745
00:38:09,180 --> 00:38:12,360
She never made a profit, and her work lay in obscurity

746
00:38:12,361 --> 00:38:14,020
for many years after her death.

747
00:38:14,760 --> 00:38:18,120
Photography would not find a welcoming place in art galleries

748
00:38:18,121 --> 00:38:19,620
until the 20th century.

749
00:38:20,420 --> 00:38:23,740
Its acceptance was due in large part to Alfred Stieglitz,

750
00:38:23,741 --> 00:38:25,740
A photographer, collector, and critic,

751
00:38:25,840 --> 00:38:29,740
Stieglitz opened a small gallery in New York in 1905

752
00:38:29,840 --> 00:38:32,740
and for 30-something years exhibited photographs side

753
00:38:32,840 --> 00:38:36,380
by side with great contemporary works of art.

754
00:38:36,480 --> 00:38:40,820
Stieglitz believed profoundly that a photograph

755
00:38:40,920 --> 00:38:44,900
could be as important a work of art as any painting

756
00:38:45,000 --> 00:38:47,800
or any sculpture that he had ever seen.

757
00:38:47,900 --> 00:38:51,160
It is clear from the works of Degas, Delacroix,

758
00:38:51,161 --> 00:38:54,200
Delacroix, and other turn-of-the-century painters

759
00:38:54,300 --> 00:38:57,500
that they drew inspiration from photography.

760
00:38:57,600 --> 00:39:00,900
The notion of photography as art came slowly.

761
00:39:01,000 --> 00:39:04,400
But by the 1880s, the camera had become a powerful political

762
00:39:04,500 --> 00:39:08,140
and social tool in the hands of some documentarians.

763
00:39:08,240 --> 00:39:09,920
The homeless and recent immigrants

764
00:39:10,020 --> 00:39:13,180
were the subjects of both Jacob Weiss, a New York photographer

765
00:39:13,280 --> 00:39:17,420
in the 1890s, and of Lewis Hine a decade later.

766
00:39:17,520 --> 00:39:20,060
Hine's photographs of children in sweatshops

767
00:39:20,061 --> 00:39:24,860
helped initiate the passage of child labor laws.

768
00:39:24,960 --> 00:39:27,600
In the Dust Bowls of the Midwest and the migrant camps

769
00:39:27,700 --> 00:39:31,140
of the Far West, Dorothea Lange, Walker Evans,

770
00:39:31,240 --> 00:39:33,180
and other government photographers

771
00:39:33,280 --> 00:39:36,880
recorded rural lives displaced by drought and poverty

772
00:39:36,980 --> 00:39:38,180
during the Great Depression.

773
00:39:45,960 --> 00:39:47,980
In its first 50 years, the camera

774
00:39:47,981 --> 00:39:50,980
had been very limited by lighting conditions.

775
00:39:51,080 --> 00:39:54,480
The invention of flash powder changed all that in 1887,

776
00:39:54,580 --> 00:39:56,380
but it was not without danger.

777
00:39:56,480 --> 00:39:58,320
It also caught people's hair on fire,

778
00:39:58,420 --> 00:40:01,820
put acrid smoke in the air, and occasionally exploded.

779
00:40:01,920 --> 00:40:03,900
The flash bulb would not come into common use

780
00:40:04,000 --> 00:40:08,660
until the 1930s to tidy things up a bit.

781
00:40:08,760 --> 00:40:11,240
With all the ingenious improvements in photography,

782
00:40:11,340 --> 00:40:14,240
why was the invention of color such a diabolically

783
00:40:14,340 --> 00:40:15,840
difficult task?

784
00:40:15,940 --> 00:40:17,440
The concept that colors could be made

785
00:40:17,441 --> 00:40:19,680
from three primary colors had been demonstrated

786
00:40:19,780 --> 00:40:23,440
by James Clark Maxwell in 1861.

787
00:40:23,540 --> 00:40:25,940
The problem was, of course, how to find a way

788
00:40:26,040 --> 00:40:29,980
to capture these three primary or secondary colors

789
00:40:30,080 --> 00:40:31,880
on a piece of film.

790
00:40:31,980 --> 00:40:34,620
Two Parisian brothers, Claude and Louis Lumiere,

791
00:40:34,720 --> 00:40:38,320
would make an ingenious breakthrough in color in 1904.

792
00:40:38,420 --> 00:40:42,000
They dyed separate batches of tiny granules of potato starch

793
00:40:42,100 --> 00:40:44,460
in one of the three primary colors.

794
00:40:44,560 --> 00:40:47,440
They combined the different color granules on film.

795
00:40:47,441 --> 00:40:51,280
The granules of potato starch acted like miniature filters.

796
00:40:51,380 --> 00:40:55,080
Their film, autochrome, rendered exquisite colors,

797
00:40:55,180 --> 00:40:59,580
but was expensive and had limited sensitivity to light.

798
00:40:59,680 --> 00:41:03,320
Only a handful of professional photographers used it.

799
00:41:03,420 --> 00:41:06,420
Eastman's research department had explored color film

800
00:41:06,520 --> 00:41:09,660
for two decades without much success.

801
00:41:09,760 --> 00:41:13,440
But the invention of 35 millimeter film, still photography

802
00:41:13,540 --> 00:41:16,740
and motion pictures would light a fire under the company.

803
00:41:16,741 --> 00:41:20,280
there was money to be made.

804
00:41:20,380 --> 00:41:23,240
I think it was a major kind of engineering task

805
00:41:23,340 --> 00:41:24,780
to figure out how to do it.

806
00:41:24,880 --> 00:41:28,680
And certainly, the incentive of 35 millimeter, I think,

807
00:41:28,780 --> 00:41:35,060
is what forced the company, forced Kodak, to find a way

808
00:41:35,160 --> 00:41:38,200
to make color practical.

809
00:41:38,300 --> 00:41:43,100
Kodak marketed a 35 millimeter color slide film in 1932.

810
00:41:43,200 --> 00:41:45,140
But Eastman did not live to see it.

811
00:41:45,141 --> 00:41:49,040
In 1931, Eastman turned 78.

812
00:41:49,140 --> 00:41:51,740
He was wealthy beyond his dreams, a friend

813
00:41:51,840 --> 00:41:53,880
of the powerful and famous.

814
00:41:53,980 --> 00:41:57,080
A man of eclectic tastes, he loved big game hunting

815
00:41:57,180 --> 00:42:01,180
and elegant dinners, camping, and classical music.

816
00:42:01,280 --> 00:42:04,400
After his 78th birthday, a party was given for Eastman

817
00:42:04,500 --> 00:42:05,600
at his home.

818
00:42:05,700 --> 00:42:09,100
Shortly after the guests left, his secretary heard a gunshot

819
00:42:09,200 --> 00:42:10,700
and rushed upstairs.

820
00:42:10,800 --> 00:42:14,140
Eastman had ended his life with a bullet to his brain.

821
00:42:14,141 --> 00:42:16,540
The father of the modern photographic process

822
00:42:16,640 --> 00:42:19,540
left a note that said only, my work is done.

823
00:42:19,640 --> 00:42:20,340
Why wait?

824
00:42:26,580 --> 00:42:28,680
Within two decades, a young man is

825
00:42:28,780 --> 00:42:33,160
keen in his business hunches as George Eastman.

826
00:42:33,260 --> 00:42:37,700
As visionary as Henry Ford would bring photography full circle

827
00:42:37,800 --> 00:42:39,600
to its beginning.

828
00:42:39,700 --> 00:42:43,140
Edwin Land, a Harvard freshman in the 1920s,

829
00:42:43,141 --> 00:42:47,340
was walking down Broadway in New York City on a college trip.

830
00:42:47,440 --> 00:42:49,140
The marquee lights dazzled him.

831
00:42:49,240 --> 00:42:51,980
So did the headlights of the cars whooshing by.

832
00:42:52,080 --> 00:42:53,480
At that moment, he got an idea that

833
00:42:53,580 --> 00:42:56,180
would set the course of his entire career.

834
00:42:56,280 --> 00:42:57,880
What if a filter could be designed

835
00:42:57,980 --> 00:43:00,800
that would cut the glare of the car's headlights?

836
00:43:00,900 --> 00:43:04,100
Land immediately took a leave of absence from Harvard.

837
00:43:04,200 --> 00:43:07,360
He worked intensely for three years in a makeshift lab

838
00:43:07,460 --> 00:43:08,800
in New York City.

839
00:43:08,900 --> 00:43:10,640
Finally, he achieved his goal.

840
00:43:10,641 --> 00:43:13,680
He was able to make an artificial material that allowed only

841
00:43:13,780 --> 00:43:16,420
light rays moving in one direction through it,

842
00:43:16,520 --> 00:43:18,320
a term called polarizing.

843
00:43:18,420 --> 00:43:20,420
The result, no more glare.

844
00:43:20,520 --> 00:43:23,680
The Kodak company immediately saw the material's potential

845
00:43:23,780 --> 00:43:25,420
as a camera filter.

846
00:43:25,520 --> 00:43:28,860
In 1934, Kodak signed a contract with Land.

847
00:43:28,960 --> 00:43:31,760
He was in business.

848
00:43:31,860 --> 00:43:33,960
Land clearly wasn't thinking about the camera

849
00:43:34,060 --> 00:43:35,460
when he made this discovery.

850
00:43:35,560 --> 00:43:37,540
Even so, he had just laid the groundwork

851
00:43:37,640 --> 00:43:39,940
for a stunning breakthrough in photography,

852
00:43:39,941 --> 00:43:43,380
A breakthrough that Land himself would make a decade later.

853
00:43:43,480 --> 00:43:45,180
Land's greatest invention would come to him

854
00:43:45,280 --> 00:43:49,040
in the same flash of insight his original idea had taken.

855
00:43:49,140 --> 00:43:52,780
Land was on a family trip to Santa Fe, New Mexico in 1944

856
00:43:52,880 --> 00:43:55,960
when his three-year-old daughter asked why she had to wait

857
00:43:56,060 --> 00:43:58,260
to see a photograph he had taken of her.

858
00:43:58,360 --> 00:44:00,560
In that moment, he visualized the elements that

859
00:44:00,660 --> 00:44:02,900
would be needed in a camera that could make a photograph

860
00:44:03,000 --> 00:44:05,300
almost instantaneously.

861
00:44:05,400 --> 00:44:08,640
He immediately realized that his life's work in plastics

862
00:44:08,641 --> 00:44:11,640
and polarizers had laid the basis for the invention

863
00:44:11,740 --> 00:44:14,140
of an instantly developing photograph,

864
00:44:14,240 --> 00:44:16,280
but it would take three years of research

865
00:44:16,380 --> 00:44:20,080
to develop the idea into a workable prototype.

866
00:44:20,180 --> 00:44:23,280
Land's film consisted of a positive and negative sheet

867
00:44:23,380 --> 00:44:26,300
with chemical seal in between.

868
00:44:26,400 --> 00:44:28,400
When the photographer pulled the exposed film

869
00:44:28,500 --> 00:44:30,200
through the rollers at the back of the camera,

870
00:44:30,300 --> 00:44:33,440
the chemicals spread across both sheets, processing

871
00:44:33,540 --> 00:44:36,800
and developing the film within minutes.

872
00:44:36,801 --> 00:44:41,100
In 1948, the first land camera was sold for $89.50

873
00:44:41,200 --> 00:44:43,240
at Jordan Marsh in Boston.

874
00:44:43,340 --> 00:44:46,440
Over 5 million were purchased in the first year.

875
00:44:46,540 --> 00:44:49,940
Today, Polaroid cameras are popular around the world.

876
00:44:50,040 --> 00:44:53,620
Oddly enough, Polaroid pictures, which are direct positives,

877
00:44:53,720 --> 00:44:56,760
are the modern equivalent of the daguerreotype.

878
00:44:56,860 --> 00:45:00,060
The story of the camera had come full circle.

879
00:45:00,160 --> 00:45:03,860
The first pictures took eight hours to make.

880
00:45:03,861 --> 00:45:07,200
Today, a picture takes a fraction of a second.

881
00:45:07,300 --> 00:45:10,200
We use photography to push nearly every scientific

882
00:45:10,300 --> 00:45:11,760
frontier.

883
00:45:11,860 --> 00:45:14,200
It's hard to imagine, at this point in time,

884
00:45:14,300 --> 00:45:16,500
a world without photography.

885
00:45:16,600 --> 00:45:22,420
I think it's changed the entire kind of human consciousness

886
00:45:22,520 --> 00:45:26,720
of the way that we look at and perceive and deal

887
00:45:26,820 --> 00:45:29,260
with the real world.

888
00:45:29,360 --> 00:45:31,020
The vision of so many centuries

889
00:45:31,021 --> 00:45:33,560
to capture the reflected image of light

890
00:45:33,660 --> 00:45:36,000
is as magical today as it was then.

891
00:45:36,100 --> 00:45:40,700
And it is possible through a simple but ingenious invention,

892
00:45:40,800 --> 00:45:41,800
the camera.

